  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much is a cord of firewood in Cleveland?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Roubic seasoned hardwood firewood runs $175–$250 per face cord, $250–$400 per half cord, and $400–$650 per full cord delivered. Premium oak adds $50–$100 over mixed-hardwood pricing. Stacking adds $50–$100. Delivery is free within 10 miles of the Auburn Township yard; longer distances run $25–$75." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What's the difference between a face cord and a full cord?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A full cord is 128 cubic feet, typically stacked 4 feet wide × 4 feet tall × 8 feet long. A face cord is approximately 42–45 cubic feet — a 4-foot-tall × 8-foot-wide stack of 16–18-inch pieces, one row deep. A face cord is roughly one-third of a full cord. A face cord is the most common residential order and lasts a fireplace 4–6 weeks of regular use during heating season." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What kind of wood burns the hottest?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Oak burns the hottest and longest — densest hardwood, slow steady burn, ideal for wood stoves and overnight fires. Maple is balanced — easier to start, slightly less BTU per cord than oak. Cherry is aromatic and burns hot but faster — better for fire pits and pizza ovens than for primary heating. Ash is easy to split and dries fast, slightly less BTU than oak. Mixed hardwood blends combine the qualities of multiple species." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How do I know if firewood is seasoned?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Three checks. First, moisture meter reading — sub-20% is well-seasoned, 20–25% is acceptable, over 25% is unseasoned. A meter costs about $20 at any hardware store. Second, visual signs — ends of the pieces show radial cracks (checks), and bark separates easily from the wood. Third, weight and sound — seasoned wood is noticeably lighter than green wood and produces a hollow ringing sound when two pieces are knocked together. Roubic firewood is seasoned 6–12 months minimum." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why does locally sourced firewood matter (EAB)?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Emerald Ash Borer is an invasive beetle that has killed an estimated 90%+ of ash trees in NE Ohio since 2008. The Ohio Department of Agriculture maintains an EAB quarantine — firewood from infested counties shouldn't move across county lines because EAB larvae can survive in the wood and infest new areas. Buying locally sourced firewood (within the same county where the wood was cut) keeps you compliant and helps slow the spread to areas that haven't been infested yet." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Do you deliver and stack firewood?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Delivery is included in the cord price; the wood is dumped at your driveway or designated drop point. Stacking at your wood-storage area is an add-on at $50–$100 depending on distance from the drop point. Tell us during the order whether you want stacking — it's the most-requested add-on." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "When should I order firewood, and how much will I need?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Order in August or early September for fall delivery — stock is abundant, pricing is stable, and the wood has time to settle before the first cold snap. Emergency orders during a January cold snap are filled when stock allows but trend toward premium pricing. For volume: homes heating primarily with a wood stove typically use 3–6 full cords per season, while fireplace-only aesthetic use runs 1–2 face cords per season." } }
